PiperSay (test build)
=====================

A test of Piper's neural voices on RISC OS: it speaks text, and says how
long that took on this computer. The voice is Southern English Female
(Piper, "low" quality, 16 kHz); text becomes phonemes through eSpeak NG.

Needs: RISC OS 5 on a machine with NEON (Raspberry Pi 2 or later). To play
the sound it needs SharedSound 1.07, StreamManager 0.03 and
SharedSoundBuffer 0.07 in System:Modules (as !SoftMIDI does).
SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager are freeware by John Duffell and can't
be included here: get ssb.zip and merge its !System into yours.
!PiperSay loads them when it's run. Without them, PiperSay can still write
the WAV file (-o).
The voice takes about 65 MB of memory once loaded.

The test
  Double-click !PiperSay (once), then open a TaskWindow (Ctrl-F12) and type
      Obey <PiperSay$Dir>.Test
  It reads TestText aloud. For each sentence it prints the number of
  phonemes, how long the speech is, how long it took to make, and the
  ratio; at the end the overall "real-time factor": under 1 means the
  computer can speak as fast as it talks. The audio is also written to
  <Wimp$ScrapDir>.PiperTest (a WAV file).

Saying something
      PiperSay -p Hello from RISC OS.
  Options:
      -p          play it
      -o file     write a WAV file
      -f file     read the text from a file
      -r 1.2      slower (0.8 faster)
      -n          no randomness (the same every time, but flatter)
      -s 42       a fixed random seed (the same every time)
      -i          show each sentence's phonemes
      -v voice    another .voice file
  Command lines are short on RISC OS; put longer text in a file.

